{"hash":"14d214aeee1cffcf","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":109.931,"said":"From the bridge, look at the island ahead. It sits between two streams of water, and the one on its far, northern side is called the Kaveri. Not the great Kaveri of the south, but a smaller river of the same name, who meets the Narmada here. How did she come to be here? Listen. Kubera, the treasurer of the gods, was a devoted worshipper of Shiva. He came to this place, set up a Shiva linga, and performed hard penance. Shiva was pleased, and made him the lord of wealth, the richest among the gods. The Matsya Purana adds that his penance here, at the meeting of the Kaveri and the Narmada, made him lord of the yakshas. And, the local story goes, so that his devotee might bathe, Shiva drew a stream from his own matted locks. That stream is the Kaveri. She flows around the Omkar hill and meets the Narmada at the sangam, a meeting the Puranas praise as famed throughout the world, and able to wash away all sins. For centuries an old temple of Kubera, called Kuber Bhandari, stood by the confluence. When the Omkareshwar dam was built, its waters rose over that shrine, and a new Kuber Bhandari temple now stands on the riverbank near Mamleshwar. There, on the night before Dhanteras, devotees keep vigil, and at dawn Kubera and Mahalakshmi are worshipped together. Walk on toward the island, and remember: even the god of wealth came here as a devotee.\n\nDid you know? On the island’s parikrama path, at the meeting of the Kaveri and the Narmada, stands Rin Mukteshwar, the Lord who frees from debt; pilgrims offer him chickpeas, praying to be released from all their debts.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1359},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1375,"end":1580}],"words":[[0.098,0.264,0,4],[0.374,0.084,5,8],[0.471,0.457,9,15],[1.084,0.228,17,21],[1.324,0.132,22,24],[1.468,0.132,25,28],[1.613,0.397,29,35],[2.021,0.397,36,41],[3.329,0.144,43,45],[3.486,0.288,46,50],[3.786,0.385,51,5